<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grigore Ureche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1590–1647</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grigore Ureche was a Moldavian boyar and chronicler who wrote the first chronicle of Moldavia in Romanian, covering 1359 to 1594. He argued for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> origin of the Romanian language and people. His work, written in a vivid narrative style, is regarded as a founding text of Romanian historiography.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grigore-ureche.jpg" alt="Grigore Ureche" width="164" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of Moldova · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stamp_of_Moldova_md437.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
